   42 ## 2.2. Data Payload Structure
   43 
   44 The 8-byte data payload's structure depends on the `Message Type`.
   45 
   46 ### `COMMAND` Payload
   47 
   48 | Byte(s)  | Field Name      | Description                                                 |
   49 |----------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   50 | `0-1`    | Command Code    | The 16-bit code of the user command (e.g., `0x2000`).         |
   51 | `2-7`    | Parameters      | Command-specific parameters. See `User_Commands/commands.md`. |
   52 
   53 ### `ACK` / `NACK` Payload
   54 
   55 | Byte(s)  | Field Name      | Description                                                 |
   56 |----------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   57 | `0-1`    | Command Code    | The 16-bit code of the command being acknowledged.          |
   58 | `2-3`    | Error Code      | For `NACK` only. A 16-bit code detailing the error. 0 for `ACK`. |
   59 | `4-7`    | Reserved        | Reserved.                                                   |
   60 
   61 ### `DATA` / `DONE` / `LOG` Payload (`Message Type == 11`)
   62 
   63 A sub-type in the first byte differentiates these messages.
   64 
   65 | Byte(s)  | Field Name      | Description                                                 |
   66 |----------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   67 | `0`      | Sub-Type        | `0x01`=DONE, `0x02`=DATA, `0x03`=LOG.                       |
   68 | `1-7`    | Sub-Payload     | Content depends on the `Sub-Type`. See `3_Application_Layer.md`. |

