    3 ---
    4 
    5 ## 2.1. CAN ID Structure (29-bit Extended)
    6 
    7 The 29-bit CAN ID is structured to convey priority, message type, source, and destination, enabling robust filtering and routing.
    8 
    9 | Bits       | Length | Field Name         | Description                                                 |
   10 |------------|--------|--------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   11 | 28-26      | 3 bits | Priority           | Message priority (0 = highest, 7 = lowest).                 |
   12 | 25-24      | 2 bits | Message Type       | Defines the purpose of the frame (see table below).         |
   13 | 23-16      | 8 bits | Destination Address| Address of the recipient node.                              |
   14 | 15-8       | 8 bits | Source Address     | Address of the sender node.                                 |
   15 | 7-0        | 8 bits | Reserved           | Reserved for future use (must be 0).                        |
   16 
   17 ### Message Types
   18 
   19 | Value (binary) | Name    | Description                                       |
   20 |----------------|---------|---------------------------------------------------|
   21 | `00`           | `COMMAND` | A command from the Conductor to an Executor.      |
   22 | `01`           | `ACK`     | Positive acknowledgment of a command.             |
   23 | `10`           | `NACK`    | Negative acknowledgment (error).                  |
   24 | `11`           | `DATA` / `DONE` / `LOG` | Asynchronous message from an Executor to the Conductor. |
   25 
   26 **Note**: `DATA`, `DONE`, and `LOG` share the same `Message Type` code. They are differentiated by a sub-type field within the data payload (see section 2.2).
   27 
   28 ### Node Addresses
   29 
   30 A table of node addresses must be maintained here.
   31 
   32 | Address (hex) | Node Name                | Description                                |
   33 |---------------|--------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
   34 | `0x01`        | Host Controller (PC)     | The main controlling computer (communicates via USB). |
   35 | `0x10`        | Conductor (This Device)  | The STM32H723 main board.                  |
   36 | `0x20`        | Dispenser Executor 1     | Executor for dispenser functions.          |
   37 | `0x30`        | Reaction Disk Executor   | Executor for the reaction disk motor.      |
   38 | ...           | ...                      | ...                                        |
   39 
   40 ## 2.2. Data Payload Structure
   41 
   42 The 8-byte data payload's structure depends on the `Message Type`.
   43 
   44 ### `COMMAND` Payload
   45 
   46 | Byte(s)  | Field Name      | Description                                                 |
   47 |----------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   48 | `0-1`    | **Low-Level** Command Code | The 16-bit code for a hardware-level action. See `5_Low_Level_Commands.md`. |
   49 | `2-7`    | Parameters      | Command-specific, low-level parameters (e.g., raw step counts). |
   50 
   51 ### `ACK` / `NACK` Payload
   52 
   53 | Byte(s)  | Field Name      | Description                                                 |
   54 |----------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   55 | `0-1`    | **Low-Level** Command Code | The 16-bit code of the command being acknowledged.          |
   56 | `2-3`    | Error Code      | For `NACK` only. A 16-bit code detailing the error. 0 for `ACK`. |
   57 | `4-7`    | Reserved        | Reserved.                                                   |
   58 
   59 ### `DATA` / `DONE` / `LOG` Payload (`Message Type == 11`)
   60 
   61 A sub-type in the first byte differentiates these messages.
   62 
