    5 ## 2.1. CAN ID Structure (29-bit Extended)
    6 
    7 The 29-bit CAN ID is structured to convey priority, message type, source, and destination, enabling robust filtering and routing.
    8 
    9 | Bits       | Length | Field Name         | Description                                                 |
   10 |------------|--------|--------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   11 | 28-26      | 3 bits | Priority           | Message priority (0 = highest, 7 = lowest).                 |
   12 | 25-24      | 2 bits | Message Type       | Defines the purpose of the frame (see table below).         |
   13 | 23-16      | 8 bits | Destination Address| Address of the recipient node.                              |
   14 | 15-8       | 8 bits | Source Address     | Address of the sender node.                                 |
   15 | 7-0        | 8 bits | Reserved           | Reserved for future use (must be 0).                        |
   16 
   17 ### Message Types
   18 
   19 | Value (binary) | Name    | Description                                       |
   20 |----------------|---------|---------------------------------------------------|
   21 | `00`           | `COMMAND` | A command from the Conductor to an Executor.      |
   22 | `01`           | `ACK`     | Positive acknowledgment of a command.             |
   23 | `10`           | `NACK`    | Negative acknowledgment (error).                  |
   24 | `11`           | `DATA` / `DONE` / `LOG` | Asynchronous message from an Executor to the Conductor. |
   25 
   26 ### Node Addresses
   27 
   28 A table of node addresses must be maintained here.
   29 
   30 | Address (hex) | Node Name                    | Description                                |
   31 |---------------|------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
   32 | `0x00`        | Broadcast                    | All nodes.                                 |
   33 | `0x01`        | Host Controller (PC)         | The main controlling computer (communicates via USB). |
   34 | `0x10`        | Conductor (Main Board)       | The STM32H723 main board.                  |
   35 | `0x20`        | Stepper Motor Control Board  | Manages stepper motors (STM32F103).        |
   36 | `0x30`        | Pump and Valve Control Board | Manages pumps and valves (STM32F103).      |
   37 | `0x40`        | Thermosensor Board           | Manages temperature sensors (STM32F103).   |
   38 | ...           | ...                          | ...                                        |
   39 
   40 ## 2.2. Data Payload & DLC
   41 
   42 The Data Length Code (DLC) and payload structure are **variable** and depend on the `Message Type`. See `6_Parameter_Packing.md` for a detailed justification.
   43 
   44 ### `COMMAND` Payload (DLC is command-specific)
   45 
   46 | Byte(s)  | Field Name      | Description                                                 |
   47 |----------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   48 | `0-1`    | **Low-Level** Command Code | The 16-bit code for a hardware-level action. See `5_Low_Level_Commands.md`. |
   49 | `2-7`    | Parameters      | Command-specific, low-level parameters. Their length defines the final DLC. |
   50 
   51 ### `ACK` / `NACK` Payload (DLC = 4)
   52 
   53 | Byte(s)  | Field Name      | Description                                                 |
   54 |----------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
   55 | `0-1`    | **Low-Level** Command Code | The 16-bit code of the command being acknowledged.          |
   56 | `2-3`    | Error Code      | For `NACK` only. A 16-bit code detailing the error. 0 for `ACK`. |
   57 
   58 ### `DATA` / `DONE` / `LOG` Payload (`Message Type == 11`)
   59 
   60 A sub-type in the first byte differentiates these messages. The DLC is variable.
   61 
   62 | Sub-Type | DLC      | Payload Description                                          |
   63 |----------|----------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
   64 | `DONE`   | 3        | `0x01` (Sub-Type) + 16-bit Low-Level Command Code            |
   65 | `DATA`   | 3-8      | `0x02` (Sub-Type) + Sequence Info + 1-6 bytes of data.       |
   66 | `LOG`    | 3-8      | `0x03` (Sub-Type) + Log Level + 1-6 bytes of message.        |
   67 
